    skos:definition "The Stage 3 document of chapter 14 is the text this page renders, section for section."@en .

pg:TopMenu a ipo:PrimaryNavigation,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Main menu"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14/document> ;
    skos:definition "The main menu is permanently visible at the top and lets the reader reach every top-level area without scrolling or opening anything first."@en .

pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Check the Noise handshake, the transport messages and the onion packet against test vectors, and read a channel on the chain: the funding output, the commitment transaction, a cooperative close and a justice transaction"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can check the Noise handshake, the transport messages and the onion packet against test vectors, and read a channel on the chain: the funding output, the commitment transaction, a cooperative close and a justice transaction."@en .

pg:Q1_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The first guarantee on the chapter's list is that no output can be spent twice in the same valid chain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q2_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The accounting guarantee says that the outputs of a transaction cannot exceed its inputs, so that value cannot be created in a payment (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q3_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A single-use seal is a thing that can be closed over a message only once, like the tamper-evident seal on a parcel, and an unspent transaction output is exactly that, because it can be spent once in a valid chain and the ..."@en .

pg:Q4_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The streaming-video example of the book is a channel in which Emma pays Fabian 0.01 millibit for each second, which is 0.00001 bitcoin or 1,000 satoshis, so an hour costs 3,600,000 satoshis, the 36 millibits that she locks, ..."@en .

pg:Q5_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The funding transaction pays into a 2-of-2 multisignature output, so that neither party can take the money without the other (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q6_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The routed payment of the book is a staircase of amounts: to deliver 1 bitcoin through three intermediaries who each want 0.001, Alice must offer Bob 1.003, Bob offers Carol 1.002, Carol offers Diana 1.001, and Diana pays Eric ..."@en .

pg:Q7_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Every hop of a routed payment must have more time than the next, so that a node that has paid forward can still collect from the one behind it: the book's HTLCs expire in 10, 9, 8 and 7 blocks, a step of one block ..."@en .

pg:Q8_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The AsciiDoc source of chapter 14 begins with the anchor [[ch12]], although the chapter is the fourteenth of the book and the file of chapter 12 begins with its own anchor [[mining]] (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q9_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The chapter introduces the Lightning Network as a proposed routed network of bidirectional payment channels, which was true of a design in 2015 and is no longer true of a network in operation (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_AssetProtocols a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Asset protocols"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AssetProtocols" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_AssetProtocols> ;
    skos:definition "RGB and Taproot Assets are the two protocols that combine the three mechanisms and that can ride on Lightning channels, and their sources are the first colored-coin overview of 2012 and the single-use-seal text of 2017 (Rosenfeld, 2012); (Todd, 2017); (RGB developers, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_AsymmetricCommitment a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The asymmetric commitment"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AsymmetricCommitment" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_AsymmetricCommitment> ;
    skos:definition "In the asymmetric commitment the holder's own output is delayed: the script has a revocation branch and a branch with 1000 and OP_CHECKSEQUENCEVERIFY, a spend with 999 confirmations was refused as non-BIP68-final and with 1000 it was accepted, and a spend without the sequence number was refused."@en .

pg:S_BalancesAfterPayment a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Balances after the payment"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BalancesAfterPayment" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_BalancesAfterPayment> ;
    skos:definition "After the payment the intermediaries are not at a loss: the balance of each intermediary rose by its fee, Alice paid 1.003 and Eric received 1 bitcoin, exactly the end state of the book's example."@en .

pg:S_BookFeeVersusNetworkFee a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The book fee versus the network fee"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BookFeeVersusNetworkFee" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_BookFeeVersusNetworkFee> ;
    skos:definition "The book's fee of 0.001 per hop is 0.3 percent of the payment over three hops, while the statistics page of the public network gives a median base fee of 500 millisatoshi and a median rate of 100 parts per million, which for the same payment of 1 bitcoin over three hops comes to about 30,001 satoshis, 0.03 percent."@en .

pg:S_BreachJusticeTransaction a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The justice transaction"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BreachJusticeTransaction" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_BreachJusticeTransaction> ;
    skos:definition "When Irene's node, restored to an old state, broadcast the revoked commitment, Hitesh's node answered with a justice transaction that spent its penalty output, as the chapter's penalty rule says."@en .

pg:S_ChainGuarantees a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chain guarantees"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ChainGuarantees" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_ChainGuarantees> ;
    skos:definition "The guarantees of the base system that the chapter lists are the bricks of every application, and four of them were run: a second spend of an output is refused, outputs cannot exceed inputs, a signed transaction does not expire, and the list itself has 18 entries (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_ChannelOnChain a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The channel on the chain"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ChannelOnChain" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_ChannelOnChain> ;
    skos:definition "What a channel looks like on the chain was read from real transactions of the test network: the funding output, a commitment with anchors, a cooperative close and a justice transaction after a breach, with the public network's size taken from a statistics page for comparison (Lightning developers, 2026); (Mempool Space, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_ChapterAnchorErratum a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The chapter anchor erratum"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ChapterAnchorErratum" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_ChapterAnchorErratum> ;
    skos:definition "The source file of chapter 14 begins with the anchor [[ch12]] while the file of chapter 12 begins with [[mining]], and the book's errata appendix, which is about the whitepaper, does not mention it."@en .

pg:S_ClientSideValidationWalk a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Client-side validation walk"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ClientSideValidationWalk" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_ClientSideValidationWalk> ;
    skos:definition "Client-side validation is done by the receiver: handed the history of three steps, an issue and two transfers, the checker recomputed every commitment against the output script on the chain and found all three equal, and a forged contract text did not match."@en .

pg:S_ColoredCoins a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Colored coins"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ColoredCoins" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_ColoredCoins> ;
    skos:definition "Colored coins are the oldest application of the chapter: marking bitcoin to stand for other assets, in the beginning by writing data into transactions (Rosenfeld, 2012), and since the proposals of single-use seals by committing off the chain to contract terms that only the parties see (Todd, 2017)."@en .

pg:S_ColoredCoinsHistory a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The history of colored coins"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ColoredCoinsHistory" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_ColoredCoinsHistory> ;
    skos:definition "Colored coins are older than the schemes the chapter names: Meni Rosenfeld's overview is dated 4 December 2012, Peter Todd's description of scalable asset transfer with single-use seals is dated 5 December 2017, and five years separate them."@en .

pg:S_CommitmentTransactionShape a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The shape of a commitment transaction"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CommitmentTransactionShape" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_CommitmentTransactionShape> ;
    skos:definition "A commitment transaction of lnd is an ordinary transaction with the BOLT 3 marks: an obscured commitment number in the lock time and the sequence, four outputs of which two are anchors of 330 satoshis, and the funding script as the witness script."@en .

pg:S_ComposedApplications a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Composed applications"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ComposedApplications" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_ComposedApplications> ;
    skos:definition "Two applications of the chapter combine several guarantees: a proof of existence needs immutability and a timestamp, and a Lighthouse-style crowdfunding transaction needs integrity and atomicity, and both were built on the test chain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_ContractStaysOffChain a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The contract stays off the chain"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ContractStaysOffChain" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_ContractStaysOffChain> ;
    skos:definition "The contract terms are not published: the 69-byte contract was committed in the key tweak, and everything the chain showed was ordinary pay-to-witness-key-hash outputs, with no token amount visible in it."@en .

pg:S_CooperativeCloseIsOneTx a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A cooperative close is one transaction"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CooperativeCloseIsOneTx" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_CooperativeCloseIsOneTx> ;
    skos:definition "A cooperative close puts one transaction on the chain without lock time or delay: the channel lived on two on-chain transactions, the funding and this closing one, whose outputs paid the final balances."@en .

pg:S_CsvDelayThousandVersus2016 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A delay of 1,000 versus 2016"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CsvDelayThousandVersus2016" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_CsvDelayThousandVersus2016> ;
    skos:definition "The book's revocable delay is 1,000 blocks, which the hand-built channel enforced to the block, while the channels of lnd on the test network reported a delay of 2016 blocks, two weeks, and a reserve of one percent of the capacity."@en .

pg:S_DoubleSpendRejected a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A double spend is rejected"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DoubleSpendRejected" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_DoubleSpendRejected> ;
    skos:definition "No output can be spent twice in one valid chain: on the test chain a second payment of the same output with a lower fee was refused as an insufficient replacement, one with a higher fee replaced the first in the node's memory pool, and once a block had confirmed the winner the loser was refused as spending a missing or spent input."@en .

pg:S_EighteenBuildingBlocks a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Eighteen building blocks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#EighteenBuildingBlocks" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_EighteenBuildingBlocks> ;
    skos:definition "The chapter opens with a list of guarantees that the base system gives to anything built on it, from no double spend and immutability to predictable issuance; counted in the saved text of the chapter there are 18 of them, and the chapter itself says the list is not complete."@en .

pg:S_EmmaFabianArithmetic a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The arithmetic of Emma and Fabian"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#EmmaFabianArithmetic" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_EmmaFabianArithmetic> ;
    skos:definition "The streaming example is plain arithmetic in satoshis: 0.01 millibit is 1,000 satoshis per second, an hour costs 3,600,000, 600 seconds cost 600,000 and leave 3,000,000, and the hand-built channel of the test chain used the same capacity of 3,600,000 satoshis and paid six seconds."@en .

pg:S_ErrataAndDrift a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Errata and drift"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ErrataAndDrift" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_ErrataAndDrift> ;
    skos:definition "Between the print and today several details of the chapter have drifted: the anchor in the source file, the word proposed for a network in operation, the date of the paper, the delay and the fees of real channels (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Poon and Dryja, 2016)."@en .

pg:S_FeeArithmetic a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The fee arithmetic"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeeArithmetic" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_FeeArithmetic> ;
    skos:definition "The routed payment of the book is a staircase of amounts: with a fee of 0.001 from each of three intermediaries the sender must offer 1.003, and on the test network of five lnd nodes with that fee the payment of 1 bitcoin cost 300,000 satoshis in fees with the hop amounts the book gives."@en .

pg:S_FullRbfIsStandard a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Full RBF is standard"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FullRbfIsStandard" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_FullRbfIsStandard> ;
    skos:definition "Release 28.0 changed the default of the mempoolfullrbf option from 0 to 1, and the option is no longer in the help of 31.1; on the test chain the higher-fee replacement of the double spend was accepted."@en .

pg:S_FundingNeedsBothKeys a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Funding needs both keys"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FundingNeedsBothKeys" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_FundingNeedsBothKeys> ;
    skos:definition "The funding output is a 2-of-2 multisignature script of 71 bytes: spent with Emma's signature alone the node refused it, and with both signatures it accepted it, so neither party can move the shared funds alone."@en .

pg:S_FundingOutputIsMultisig a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The funding output is a multisignature"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FundingOutputIsMultisig" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_FundingOutputIsMultisig> ;
    skos:definition "The lnd funding output is a pay-to-witness-script-hash of a 2-of-2 multisignature script, and BOLT 3 sorts the two keys, which the key order in the script on the test chain showed."@en .

pg:S_HashIsRipemdOfSha a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "RIPEMD-160 of SHA-256"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HashIsRipemdOfSha" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_HashIsRipemdOfSha> ;
    skos:definition "The book writes HASH160 of H in the script but the payment hash is the 32-byte SHA-256 of the secret: the script holds the 20-byte RIPEMD-160 of that hash, as BOLT 3 writes it, and the hand-built contract used it."@en .

pg:S_HashTimeLockContracts a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Hash time lock contracts"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HashTimeLockContracts" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_HashTimeLockContracts> ;
    skos:definition "The hash time lock contract turns two parties' channel into a link of a chain: a secret opens it, a time closes it."@en .

pg:S_HoldInvoiceInFlight a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A hold invoice in flight"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HoldInvoiceInFlight" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_HoldInvoiceInFlight> ;
    skos:definition "A hold invoice keeps the payment in flight: while the receiver withheld the secret every channel along the route carried a pending HTLC, releasing the secret settled it back along the route, and cancelling the invoice returned all balances unchanged."@en .

pg:S_HtlcSecretBranch a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The HTLC secret branch"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HtlcSecretBranch" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_HtlcSecretBranch> ;
    skos:definition "The hash branch of the HTLC pays whoever shows the secret and the receiver's signature: the right secret with the receiver's key was accepted, a wrong secret was refused, the right secret with the payer's key was refused, and the secret has 32 bytes."@en .

pg:S_HtlcTimeoutBranch a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The HTLC timeout branch"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HtlcTimeoutBranch" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_HtlcTimeoutBranch> ;
    skos:definition "The time branch refunds the payer only after the lock height: the refund was refused as non-final one block before the lock and accepted at it, 19 blocks after the funding, and a refund transaction with a lower lock time than the script's was refused by the script."@en .

pg:S_InvoiceStructure a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The structure of an invoice"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#InvoiceStructure" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_InvoiceStructure> ;
    skos:definition "The invoice is the payment request of the book: a bech32 string of the BOLT 11 format whose human-readable part names the network and the amount, and whose fields hold the payment hash, the payment secret and the expiry; the secret released later hashed to the payment hash."@en .

pg:S_InvoiceVector a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The invoice vector"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#InvoiceVector" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_InvoiceVector> ;
    skos:definition "The BOLT 11 example for a cup of coffee was parsed by the Python decoder: the prefix lnbc2500u means 2500 micro-bitcoin, which is 250,000,000 millisatoshi or 250,000 satoshis, with a 32-byte payment hash, an expiry of 60 seconds and a 65-byte signature."@en .

pg:S_LighthouseCrowdfund a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Lighthouse crowdfunding"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LighthouseCrowdfund" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_LighthouseCrowdfund> ;
    skos:definition "A crowdfunding transaction in the style of Lighthouse combines integrity and atomicity: each pledger signs only his own input and the one fixed output with ANYONECANPAY, so with one pledge the node refuses the transaction for lack of value, with both it accepts it, and a change of the output breaks the signatures."@en .

pg:S_LightningNetwork a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The Lightning Network"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LightningNetwork" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_LightningNetwork> ;
    skos:definition "The Lightning Network links channels into a routed network, first described by Joseph Poon and Thaddeus Dryja (Poon and Dryja, 2016) and specified today in the BOLT documents (Lightning developers, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_LightningNoLongerProposed a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Lightning is no longer proposed"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LightningNoLongerProposed" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_LightningNoLongerProposed> ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls the Lightning Network a proposed network; the same saved page of the statistics service counts its channels and nodes, and Wikipedia says that Lightning Labs launched it in 2018."@en .

pg:S_Limits a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Limits of the experiments"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Limits" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_Limits> ;
    skos:definition "A research record is only as good as its honesty about what was not done, and the ten items that the sandbox could not run are listed with their reasons (Lightning developers, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_LndDefaults a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The lnd defaults"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LndDefaults" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_LndDefaults> ;
    skos:definition "The lnd of the test network advertises features that the chapter does not know, among them anchor commitments with zero-fee HTLC transactions, and its sample configuration sets a base fee of 1000 millisatoshi, a timelock delta of 80 and a minimum channel size of 20,000 satoshis."@en .

pg:S_MainnetGraphSize a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The size of the public graph"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MainnetGraphSize" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_MainnetGraphSize> ;
    skos:definition "The public network that the book calls proposed has, on the statistics page, 32,518 channels, 16,225 nodes and a capacity of 375,370,668,018 satoshis on 30 August 2026, which is about 3,753.7 bitcoin."@en .

pg:S_NewMachinery a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "New machinery"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NewMachinery" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_NewMachinery> ;
    skos:definition "New machinery in Bitcoin Core and in the Lightning ecosystem since the print is covered here: package relay and anchors, default replacement, proposals that are still drafts, the other implementations and the features of lnd (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024); (Harding and Todd, 2015); (Zhao, 2024)."@en .

pg:S_NoiseHandshakeVector a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The Noise handshake vector"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NoiseHandshakeVector" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_NoiseHandshakeVector> ;
    skos:definition "The transport of BOLT 8 is a Noise handshake of three acts: from the published test keys a Python initiator produced the 50-byte first act and the 66-byte third act of the vector and the sending key 969ab31b."@en .

pg:S_NonExpiration a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A transaction does not expire"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NonExpiration" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_NonExpiration> ;
    skos:definition "A valid transaction does not expire: a payment signed at height 301 and kept back was still accepted by the node at height 451, 150 blocks later, because nothing in it or in the rules had changed, which is the property that lets a channel party keep a signed transaction for weeks."@en .

pg:S_OneWayChannel a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The one-way channel"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OneWayChannel" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_OneWayChannel> ;
    skos:definition "The one-way channel of Emma and Fabian shows the funding output, the signed commitments and the refund with a lock time, and the numbers of the book were reproduced and the contracts tested on the private chain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Decker and Wattenhofer, 2015)."@en .

pg:S_OnionFixedSize a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The fixed size of the onion"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OnionFixedSize" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_OnionFixedSize> ;
    skos:definition "The packet does not shorten along the path: built with one, two or five hops it is always 1366 bytes, one version byte, a 33-byte key, 1300 bytes of payloads and a 32-byte HMAC, so a node cannot read its place in the path from the size."@en .

pg:S_OnionIntegrityByHmac a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Onion integrity by HMAC"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OnionIntegrityByHmac" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_OnionIntegrityByHmac> ;
    skos:definition "The first hop checks the HMAC over the routing bytes and the associated data with a key from its shared secret: the vector packet passed, and the same packet with one bit flipped at the start or at the end of the 1300 bytes failed."@en .

pg:S_OnionPacketVector a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The onion packet vector"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OnionPacketVector" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_OnionPacketVector> ;
    skos:definition "The Sphinx onion of BOLT 4 was rebuilt from the session key, the five hop keys and the payloads of the published vector and equalled the vector's packet byte for byte; its length is 1366 bytes."@en .

pg:S_OtherImplementations a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Other implementations"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OtherImplementations" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_OtherImplementations> ;
    skos:definition "The book says that at least five teams implement the Lightning Network; the saved READMEs show Core Lightning, Eclair and the Lightning Development Kit besides lnd, the only one that was run, in version 0.21.3-beta."@en .

pg:S_OutputsCannotExceedInputs a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Outputs cannot exceed inputs"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OutputsCannotExceedInputs" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_OutputsCannotExceedInputs> ;
    skos:definition "The accounting building block says that the outputs of a transaction cannot exceed its inputs: a transaction whose outputs were larger than the value it spent was refused by the node with the reason bad-txns-in-belowout, the same reason that stopped a pledge which lacked the second pledger's input."@en .

pg:S_PayToContractTweak a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The pay to contract tweak"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PayToContractTweak" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_PayToContractTweak> ;
    skos:definition "Pay to contract adds to a public key a tweak computed from the contract, so that the output commits to the contract without showing it: recomputed here the tweaked key equalled the plain key plus the tweak times the generator point, the output was spent with the tweaked key and refused with the plain key, and the reveal fitted only the true contract."@en .

pg:S_PaymentChannels a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Payment channels"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PaymentChannels" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_PaymentChannels> ;
    skos:definition "A payment channel moves many payments between two parties while the chain sees two transactions, the funding and the settlement, and the book builds it up in three steps: the one-way channel with refund timelocks, the asymmetric revocable commitment, and the hash time lock contract (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_PenaltyTakesEverything a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The penalty takes everything"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PenaltyTakesEverything" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_PenaltyTakesEverything> ;
    skos:definition "The book's numbers show why cheating does not pay: of 10 coins the cheater would gain 2 by going back from 7 and 3 to 5 and 5, but if he is caught the honest party takes all 10, 3 more than he was owed, and the penalty spend itself was confirmed on the test chain."@en .

pg:S_PlatformGuarantees a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Platform guarantees"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PlatformGuarantees" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_PlatformGuarantees> ;
    skos:definition "The chapter starts from the view that Bitcoin is a platform: a list of guarantees such as no double spend, immutability, accounting and non-expiration can be combined into applications, and the book names the notary and the crowdfunding transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_ProofOfExistence a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Proof of existence"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ProofOfExistence" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_ProofOfExistence> ;
    skos:definition "A proof of existence needs only a commitment in a transaction: the 32-byte SHA-256 of a document was written as an OP_RETURN output of value zero, the node confirmed it in a block, and the output script read back as OP_RETURN followed by exactly that hash, so the document existed no later than that block."@en .

pg:S_ProposalsStillDrafts a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The proposals are still drafts"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ProposalsStillDrafts" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_ProposalsStillDrafts> ;
    skos:definition "The covenant and signature proposals that Lightning designers wait for, BIP 118 and BIP 119, and the topology and anchor rules of BIPs 431 and 433 are still marked as drafts in the saved BIP files, and Optech calls eltoo a proposed mechanism."@en .

pg:S_RefundTimelockLadder a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The refund timelock ladder"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RefundTimelockLadder" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_RefundTimelockLadder> ;
    skos:definition "With absolute lock times the later commitment can be broadcast first: the six commitments had lock times from 488 down to 483, all were refused as non-final at the start, at height 483 only the sixth was accepted, and once it was mined the earlier ones were refused because their input was gone; 30 days at 144 blocks a day are the book's 4,320 blocks."@en .

pg:S_RevocableCommitments a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Revocable commitments"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RevocableCommitments" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_RevocableCommitments> ;
    skos:definition "The asymmetric revocable commitment replaces the ladder of lock times by a penalty: an output delayed by a relative lock time and a revocation key of which each party holds half, which together make cheating costly, as the book's exchanges Hitesh and Irene show (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Friedenbach et al., 2015)."@en .

pg:S_RevocationKeyVector a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The revocation key vector"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RevocationKeyVector" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_RevocationKeyVector> ;
    skos:definition "The BOLT 3 derivation of the revocation key needs both the revocation base secret and the per-commitment secret: from the published secrets the Python code gave the local key, the revocation public key and the revocation private key of the appendix."@en .

pg:S_RevocationNeedsBothHalves a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Revocation needs both halves"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RevocationNeedsBothHalves" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_RevocationNeedsBothHalves> ;
    skos:definition "The penalty path needs the revocation private key, which is the sum of two halves: with one half the node refused the spend, with both halves it accepted it and the spend was confirmed, and its witness had three items of 72, 1 and 77 bytes in the recorded run."@en .

pg:S_RgbAndTaprootAssets a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "RGB and Taproot Assets"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RgbAndTaprootAssets" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_RgbAndTaprootAssets> ;
    skos:definition "RGB and Taproot Assets are the two protocols the chapter names; the saved pages of both still describe them, the Optech topic on client-side validation lists both, and the book's own note that Taproot Assets is not part of the Bitcoin protocol is in the saved text."@en .

pg:S_RoutedPayment a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The routed payment"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RoutedPayment" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_RoutedPayment> ;
    skos:definition "The routed payment of the book was run on five lnd nodes with the book's fees, and its amounts, timeouts and balances, the hold invoice and the invoice itself are compared with the book's steps (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Lightning developers, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_SealsAndContracts a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Seals and contracts"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SealsAndContracts" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_SealsAndContracts> ;
    skos:definition "The three mechanisms of modern colored coins are an output that can be spent once, a public key that commits to a contract, and a receiver who checks the history, and each was built by hand and tested against the node (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Wuille et al., 2020)."@en .

pg:S_SinceThePrint a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Since the print"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SinceThePrint" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_SinceThePrint> ;
    skos:definition "Between the print of the book and October 2026 the Lightning Network went into operation, Bitcoin Core gained package relay, version-3 transactions and pay to anchor, replacement became the default, and several details of the chapter turned out to differ from the real software (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024); (Mempool Space, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_StateIndexBits a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The 48-bit state index"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#StateIndexBits" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_StateIndexBits> ;
    skos:definition "A state number of 48 bits allows 2 to the power 48, 281,474,976,710,656 states, about 2.8 times 10 to the 14, and the per-commitment secret of the highest index is derived from the seed by the shachain steps, which gave the BOLT 3 value for a seed of all ff bytes."@en .

pg:S_TimelockStaircase a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The timelock staircase"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TimelockStaircase" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_TimelockStaircase> ;
    skos:definition "Every hop must have more time than the next: the book's timeouts of 10, 9, 8 and 7 blocks follow from a delta of one block, while the lnd nodes used the delta of 80 blocks that is their default, so the expiries on the real route stepped down by 80."@en .

pg:S_TransportAndOnion a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Transport and onion"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TransportAndOnion" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_TransportAndOnion> ;
    skos:definition "The privacy of Lightning rests on an encrypted transport and an onion of layered messages, and both were recomputed in Python from the published test vectors of BOLT 8, BOLT 4 and BOLT 11 (Lightning developers, 2026); (Danezis and Goldberg, 2009)."@en .

pg:S_TransportMessageVector a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The transport message vector"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TransportMessageVector" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_TransportMessageVector> ;
    skos:definition "After the handshake each message is an encrypted length and an encrypted body: the 5 bytes hello became 39 bytes, 2 plus 16 plus 5 plus 16, and equalled the ciphertext of the vector."@en .

pg:S_TrucAndAnchors a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "TRUC and anchors"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TrucAndAnchors" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_TrucAndAnchors> ;
    skos:definition "Since Bitcoin Core 28.0 a version-3 parent with a zero-value pay-to-anchor output can be sent with its child as a package: alone the parent was refused for its minimum relay fee, together the package was accepted with an effective rate of about 10.9 satoshis per virtual byte."@en .

pg:S_UtxoIsASingleUseSeal a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "An output is a single-use seal"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#UtxoIsASingleUseSeal" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_UtxoIsASingleUseSeal> ;
    skos:definition "A single-use seal is something that can be closed to one message only once, and an unspent output is exactly that: in a chain of three seals each earlier output was spent by the next transaction, and the three seal outpoints were three different ones."@en .

pg:S_WhatWasNotExecuted a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "What was not executed"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WhatWasNotExecuted" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_WhatWasNotExecuted> ;
    skos:definition "Ten parts of the chapter's subject were not executed, each with its reason, among them the public network, the other implementations, a watchtower, an HTLC expiring on the chain and the signature of the lnd release."@en .

pg:S_WhitepaperDate a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The date of the whitepaper"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WhitepaperDate" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14#S_WhitepaperDate> ;
    skos:definition "The book dates the first description to February 2015; the saved Wikipedia text agrees and cites a copy archived on 28 February 2015, while the draft of the paper kept at lightning.network is version 0.5.9.2 of 14 January 2016, 320 days later."@en .

pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Explain how an output works as a single-use seal, how a contract stays off the chain, and how colored coins and later asset protocols use this"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can explain how an output works as a single-use seal, how a contract stays off the chain, and how colored coins and later asset protocols use this."@en .

pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Explain what the chain guarantees to an application (no double spend, outputs cannot exceed inputs, no expiry) and how applications such as proof of existence and crowdfunding are composed from these building blocks"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Understand ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can explain what the chain guarantees to an application (no double spend, outputs cannot exceed inputs, no expiry) and how applications such as proof of existence and crowdfunding are composed from these building blocks."@en .

pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Work through a payment channel in satoshis: funding by both keys, refunds with falling lock times, revocable commitments with a penalty, and the HTLC with its secret and timeout branches"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch14/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can work through a payment channel in satoshis: funding by both keys, refunds with falling lock times, revocable commitments with a penalty, and the HTLC with its secret and timeout branches."@en .

pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
